# Git: fork only (GuyZivRH), not upstream

This checkout tracks **two remotes**. Agents must treat them as follows.

| Remote | URL | Role |
|--------|-----|------|
| `fork` | `https://github.com/GuyZivRH/agent-eval-harness.git` | **Allowed** — default for branch work, commits, push |
| `origin` | `git@github.com:opendatahub-io/agent-eval-harness.git` | **Upstream read-only** for this project — do **not** push or land work here |

## Do

- Commit and push only to **`fork`** (`GuyZivRH/agent-eval-harness`).
- Base new branches on **`fork/main`** (or an existing fork feature branch), not on `origin/main`.
- When comparing “how far from main”, prefer **`fork/main`** unless the user explicitly names upstream.
- `git fetch origin` is OK for awareness; do not update upstream with our commits.

## Don’t

- Do **not** `git push origin …` (especially not `origin/main` or any `opendatahub-io` branch).
- Do **not** open or merge PRs into **`opendatahub-io/agent-eval-harness`** unless the user explicitly overrides this rule in the current message.
- Do **not** rebase/merge our Forge/OpenShell work onto upstream `origin/main` as the default integration target — use **`fork/main`**.

## Examples

```bash
# ✅ GOOD
git fetch fork
git rebase fork/main
git push -u fork HEAD

# ❌ BAD
git push origin HEAD
git push origin main
gh pr create --repo opendatahub-io/agent-eval-harness …
```

If the user says “upstream” or `opendatahub-io` without an explicit override, remind them this project is **fork-only** and continue on `fork`.
